Skip the #BP self-test, which tests a handler this guest does not install.

int3_selftest() registers a die notifier, executes an int3, and requires the
notifier to have seen it:

    BUG_ON(register_die_notifier(&int3_exception_nb));
    int3_selftest_asm(&val);
    BUG_ON(val != 0x1234);

A cooperative guest runs on the host's IDT on purpose -- see the note on
idt_setup_early_traps in keep-host-idt.diff -- so do_int3() is never installed
and no notifier can run. The int3 goes to the host's stub instead, which is
exactly what the design intends and exactly what this test is written to
detect the absence of.

It stopped the boot at 97,340,568 instructions, reported as vector 3 at
int3_selftest_asm+0x1, which is the int3 itself.

Skipping the self-test is not the same as skipping what it tests. Nothing here
patches an int3 into anything. The test exists to prove the #BP path is
reachable before text_poke_bp() begins depending on it, and text_poke_bp() is
machinery for patching live code while other processors may be executing it --
an smp_call_function affair. This guest is pinned to one processor and stepped,
and the alternatives it applies are applied before anything else runs.

Placed at the call rather than inside the function so that the reason lives
where the decision is, and so a kernel booted on real hardware still runs the
test unmodified.

--- a/arch/x86/kernel/alternative.c
+++ b/arch/x86/kernel/alternative.c
@@ -6,6 +6,7 @@
 #include <linux/vmalloc.h>
 #include <linux/memory.h>
 #include <linux/execmem.h>
+#include <linux/console.h>	/* co_colinux_guest */

 #include <asm/text-patching.h>
 #include <asm/insn.h>
@@ -2383,7 +2384,25 @@
 {
 	u64 ibt;

-	int3_selftest();
+	/*
+	 * int3_selftest() checks that the kernel's own #BP handler works, by
+	 * executing an int3 and requiring a die notifier to have seen it.
+	 *
+	 * A cooperative guest does not have that handler. It runs on the
+	 * host's IDT deliberately -- see the note on idt_setup_early_traps --
+	 * so the int3 goes to the host's stub, no notifier runs, and the
+	 * BUG_ON(val != 0x1234) immediately after would fire on a facility
+	 * this guest has no use for and has not installed.
+	 *
+	 * Skipping a self-test is not the same as skipping the thing it
+	 * tests. Nothing here patches an int3 into anything; the test exists
+	 * to prove the handler is reachable before text_poke_bp() starts
+	 * relying on it, and text_poke_bp() is a smp_call_function affair for
+	 * patching live code on other processors, which this guest, pinned to
+	 * one and running stepped, does not do.
+	 */
+	if (!co_colinux_guest)
+		int3_selftest();

